{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7982166,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"landscaping\",\"short_title\":\"best-edging-options-garden-beds-guide\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:12:22\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-08 18:12:22\",\"author\":\"Lisa Thompson\",\"img\":null,\"title\":\"Best Edging Options for Garden Beds\",\"description\":\"Compare steel, stone, and plastic garden bed edging options with installation tips for a professional landscape look.\",\"url\":null,\"body\":\"<h2>Why Garden Edging Matters</h2><p>Proper edging creates clean lines between lawn and beds, prevents grass invasion, and gives your landscape a professional, finished look. The right edging also reduces maintenance time.</p><h2>Material Comparison</h2><h3>Steel Edging</h3><p>Cor-ten steel edging lasts 20+ years and develops a natural rust patina. Install 6-8 inches deep to block grass runners. Costs $3-5 per linear foot but virtually zero maintenance afterward.</p><h3>Natural Stone</h3><p>Flagstone or fieldstone provides a timeless look. Set stones 4-6 inches below grade so the mower passes over the top. Best for curved, informal bed designs.</p><h3>Plastic Edging</h3><p>Affordable and easy to install, but typically lasts only 3-5 years before becoming brittle. Choose heavy-duty 0.5mm+ thickness for best durability.</p><h2>Installation Tips</h2><p>Cut a clean edge with a half-moon edger or spade before installing. Remove all grass and roots from the trench. Backfill firmly on both sides to prevent shifting. A slight crown (1/4 inch above grade) sheds water away from the bed.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":null,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-08 18:12:22\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}